# Measurement data licence

The telemetry in this directory is licensed under the Creative Commons
Attribution 4.0 International Licence (CC BY 4.0).

Full text: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode
Summary:   https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/

You are free to share and adapt this data for any purpose, including
commercially, provided you give appropriate credit, link to the licence, and
indicate whether changes were made.

## What this covers

Every file under `logs/`: the per-campaign directories with their inference,
power and per-core frequency streams and the fused `*_merged_analysis.csv`, the
idle and residency profiles, the indoor ambient record under `logs/ambient/`,
and `MANIFEST.md`.

The software that produced and analyses this data is separately licensed under
the MIT Licence; see `../LICENSE`.

## How to attribute

Cite the paper. `CITATION.cff` in the repository root carries the machine-
readable form, and GitHub renders a "Cite this repository" button from it.

## Read this before you use the data

`MANIFEST.md` is not optional reading. Thirteen 48-hour campaigns were run and
seven feed the reported results; two of the excluded ones silently executed
without an effective frequency cap and are superseded by verified
re-executions. Which campaigns belong in an analysis, and why, is recorded
there. Analysing the wrong subset is the easiest mistake to make with this
data, and it is one we made ourselves before the manifest existed.

Two further cautions, both learned the hard way and both documented in
`../CLAUDE.md`:

- Filenames encode the target, not the achievement. Every file is named
  `*_48.00h_*` regardless of what completed. One run carries 40.1 h of power
  telemetry rather than 48. Check spans before trusting a campaign.
- `analysis_output/combined_dataset.csv.gz` carries a 60-sample moving average
  and drops rows whose power sample is missing. It is built for figures. Do not
  count events from it; recompute from the per-campaign
  `*_merged_analysis.csv`, which is one row per inference event.
